Chapter 22 - push me
Gym Gamma had been cleared. The main floor was open, recovery mats stacked at the edges, Recovery Girl’s station already set up along the far wall. Curtis stood in the center in his own workout clothes—black compression shirt and pants, braids tied back, scarred hand loose at his side. No costume. No armor. Just him.
Nezu, Aizawa, and All Might watched from the raised observation platform. Aizawa’s bandages were still fresh. All Might’s arms were crossed. Nezu’s smile was small and sharp.
The doors opened. Students filed into the stands in clusters—Class 1-A near the front, 1-B right beside them, third-years higher up. Mirio was already leaning on the railing, grinning. Bakugo stood with his hands in his pockets, explosions popping softly. Midoriya had his notebook out. The rest filled in around them, phones lowered, voices dropping as they got a clear look at the exchange student who had fought a Nomu and was now daring the entire school to move him.
Curtis rolled his neck once. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet. He faced the entrance where the first challengers were already gathering and waited.
Curtis’s voice carried across the gym, even and clear.
“Out of all of you, who’s the strongest?” He scanned the stands, then the cluster waiting at the entrance. “My sights are on the first-years who are actually in the Festival. But I see third-years who look like they want a piece of me too.”
The stands stirred. Mirio’s grin widened. He hopped the railing and landed light on the floor, one hand raised. “That’d be me, probably! Mirio Togata, third-year. If you’re offering, I’ll take the first slot!”
Bakugo’s palms cracked louder. “Hell no. I’m going first. Move, upperclassman.”
Todoroki stepped forward from the 1-A group without a word. Monoma was already arguing with Kendo about the order. Aizawa’s visible eye narrowed on the platform. All Might watched, arms still crossed. Nezu simply nodded once, as if the response was exactly what he’d expected.
Curtis set his feet, scarred hand loose, and looked at the growing line. “Line up however you want. First one who pushes me the way that Nomu did ends this. Rest of you can watch.”
Mirio was already walking toward the center, smile bright. The gym settled. The challenges had officially started.
Curtis crossed the floor and stopped directly in front of Mirio. The height difference was immediate. Curtis looked down. Mirio looked up, then let out a low whistle.
“Six-three? Damn. They weren’t kidding about the foreigner being built different.” He grinned, completely unbothered. “I’m Mirio. You’re Beardy, right? The one who fought the Nomu.”
“Curtis.” His tone stayed even. “You sure you want first?”
“Wouldn’t miss it.” Mirio rolled his shoulders, still smiling. “Fair warning—my quirk’s kind of annoying. Try not to get frustrated if you can’t touch me.”
Curtis nodded once. The scarred hand flexed. Around them the stands had gone quieter, everyone leaning in. On the platform Aizawa’s eye stayed half-lidded. All Might watched without comment. Nezu’s smile hadn’t moved.
“Whenever you’re ready,” Curtis said.
Mirio’s grin sharpened. He set his feet. The first challenge of the afternoon stood face to face in the center of Gym Gamma, and the rest of U.A. held its breath.
Curtis stepped in and threw a clean right. His fist passed straight through Mirio’s chest like the third-year wasn’t solid. No impact. No resistance. Mirio just stood there, grinning.
“Oh?”
Curtis’s mouth curved. The smile reached both eyes. “I know. You’re Vault Boy. I’ve seen you on the cover of those Fallout games in the USA. Your power’s like glitching out. Bugging through walls and stuff.”
Mirio blinked, then laughed so hard he almost dropped his stance. “Vault Boy? That’s a new one! It’s Permeation—I can slip through anything solid, including people. Not exactly a video game glitch, but I’ll take the comparison!”
The stands erupted. Mina was already dying. Kaminari yelled “He called him Vault Boy!” Bakugo looked personally offended by the reference. Even Aizawa’s visible eye had narrowed in something close to amusement.
Curtis reset his feet, still smiling. “Permeation. Got it. Annoying, like you said.” He rolled his neck. “Come on, then. Show me how far the glitch goes.”
Mirio’s grin turned sharper. He dropped into a ready stance, fully permeable, and the real exchange finally started.
Curtis kept swinging. Fist, elbow, knee—each one passed through empty space. He wasn’t trying to land them anymore. He was listening. Every time Mirio went fully permeable the sound of his breathing cut out. A half-second of silence. Then it returned when he solidified to strike.
Mirio slipped a hook and drove a clean punch into Curtis’s ribs. It connected. Curtis barely shifted. He absorbed it, scarred hand catching Mirio’s wrist on the way out, and smiled.
“That’s enough. You’ll die before you even push me.” His voice stayed even. “All I have to do is make sure you don’t turn your quirk off. You stop breathing every time you use it. I can hear when you stop.”
The gym went quiet. Mirio’s eyes widened. He was still smiling, but the grin had changed—sharper, more serious. He stepped back, fully solid, and rubbed the wrist Curtis had caught.
“You picked that up mid-fight.” He laughed once, short. “Okay. Yeah. That’s… actually terrifying. And fair.” He raised both hands. “I’m out. You win this one. Not gonna suffocate for a Festival spot.”
On the platform Aizawa’s eye had narrowed to a slit. All Might’s arms were still crossed, expression unreadable. The stands buzzed. Curtis rolled his shoulders, the smile fading back to neutral, and looked toward the next person already stepping onto the floor. The first challenge was over. Mirio hadn’t pushed him. The line was still long.
Curtis turned toward the platform, voice carrying.
“So, Mr. Principal, All Might. I just beat what U.A. considers the best of—”
The explosion hit him clean in the side of the head. Bakugo had crossed the floor at full burst, palms already detonating. Curtis staggered. Bakugo didn’t let him recover. He tackled him to the mat, mounted, and started raining blasts point-blank.
“I’m not letting you quit, you son of a bitch!” Each word came with another explosion. “You’re going to participate so I can kick your ass on the grand stage!”
The stands erupted. Kirishima yelled Bakugo’s name. Aizawa’s capture weapon twitched but didn’t fire. All Might’s eyes narrowed. Nezu simply watched.
Curtis got his arms up. The blasts scorched the compression shirt and rocked his head against the mat, but he didn’t tap. He caught one of Bakugo’s wrists mid-swing, the scarred hand locking tight, and looked up through the smoke with both eyes lit.
“You done announcing it,” he said, voice steady under the noise, “or are you actually trying to push me?”
Bakugo’s grin was all teeth. The next explosion was already building in his free palm. The second challenge had started without a countdown.
Curtis locked both of Bakugo’s wrists in his hands and held them apart, stopping the next blast before it could build. Smoke curled between them. He looked up from the mat, heterochromia steady.
“Do you hate me?”
Bakugo’s teeth were bared. He was still a first-year—same class, same Festival—but the rage didn’t care about year. “I hate that you’re trying to run! You’re strong and you’re gonna stand there and quit so the rest of us look better? Fuck that!” He strained against the grip, palms crackling. “I don’t care if you’re a freak or a cryptid or whatever. You’re in 1-A. You fight. Then I beat you where everyone can see it!”
Curtis held the wrists a second longer, reading the answer in the snarl more than the words. He wasn’t hated. He was being refused an exit. He released one wrist, let the explosion detonate harmlessly past his head, and drove an open palm into Bakugo’s chest that sent the blonde skidding off him.
“Then hit harder,” Curtis said, already rising. “Because right now you’re not pushing me. You’re just loud.”
Bakugo caught himself, grinning through the smoke, and launched again. The stands roared. On the platform Aizawa still hadn’t moved to stop it. The second challenge kept going.
Curtis tightened his grip. Both of Bakugo’s wrists creaked under the pressure. The explosions died instantly. Bakugo’s eyes widened as the bones ground together.
“I can snap your wrists,” Curtis said, voice low and even. “You will never wield your quirk again. Is making me participate worth your hero career?” He held the stare. “Think for once in your life. Is getting me in the Festival worth being crippled?”
The gym went dead silent. Bakugo’s breath hitched. Sweat rolled down his face. He could feel exactly how little effort it would take. One twist. Career over.
On the platform Aizawa’s capture weapon finally shifted. All Might’s hands had uncrossed. Nezu’s smile was gone. In the stands Kirishima had gone pale. Midoriya’s notebook hung forgotten.
Bakugo’s jaw clenched so hard it shook. The rage was still there. So was the calculation. He forced the words out through his teeth.
“…Let go.”
Curtis held for one more second, then released. Bakugo stumbled back, clutching his wrists, palms dark. He didn’t launch again. He just stood there, breathing hard, glaring, the threat still ringing in the sudden quiet.
Curtis rolled his shoulders and looked toward the rest of the line. “Next. Or are we done pretending any of you can push me today?”
No one moved right away. The second challenge had ended without a winner. The scarred hand that had just threatened to end a career hung loose at Curtis’s side while the entire gym waited to see who, if anyone, would step forward next.
Todoroki stepped onto the floor without a word. The half-and-half hair and the burn scar were clear under the gym lights. He stopped a few meters from Curtis, hands in his pockets, expression flat.
“I’ll go.”
Curtis nodded once. “Whenever you’re ready.”
Todoroki’s right side flared. Ice erupted across the mats in a wide wave, racing for Curtis’s legs. Curtis didn’t Blink. He simply stepped through the leading edge, boots cracking the forming sheet, and closed the distance before the wave could lock him. Todoroki’s eyes narrowed. He switched—left side igniting, a controlled burst of flame forcing Curtis to angle away.
They traded. Ice to cut space. Fire to punish the approach. Curtis slipped both, scarred hand deflecting a frozen shard, shoulder rolling under a flame jet. He never countered with full force. Just moved, read, and stayed inside Todoroki’s range without taking real damage.
“You’re holding the fire back,” Curtis said, even. “Same as the USJ. You planning to push me with half of what you’ve got?”
Todoroki’s jaw tightened. The temperature around them dropped and spiked at the same time. He didn’t answer. He just attacked again—faster, colder, the fire still restrained—while the stands watched in silence and Curtis continued to meet every strike without ever looking like he was being pushed.
Curtis sighed and caught the next ice spike bare-handed, crushing it. He didn’t advance.
“So why do you want me to participate, Shoto?”
Todoroki’s next attack stalled. The fire on his left side flickered lower. He studied Curtis for a second, breath misting in the cold he’d created.
“Because if you leave, the Festival becomes a measuring stick that doesn’t include the highest mark.” His voice stayed quiet. “I intend to win. I intend to do it with my right side. If you’re not there, I won’t know whether that win means anything against someone who already fought a Nomu and walked away.” He met Curtis’s eyes. “I don’t care about your justice speech. I care about the result. Stay so the result is real.”
Curtis was quiet. The crushed ice melted in his grip. Around them the gym stayed hushed. Todoroki’s stance didn’t drop. He was still ready. He just wasn’t hiding the reason anymore.
Curtis rolled his neck once. “Honest. More than most.” He set his feet again. “Then show me the fire you’re not using. Or this ends the same as the others.”
Todoroki’s left side flared hotter. The real exchange resumed.
Curtis brushed aside another wave of ice and stopped advancing.
“You’re wasting my time, then.” His voice stayed flat. “You want to beat me. Use both sides at full power. Otherwise you’re a hypocrite, and you give your father more control over your fate than he already has.”
The words landed harder than the ice. Todoroki’s eyes widened a fraction. The fire on his left side guttered, then surged—uncontrolled for a second—before he forced it back down. His jaw locked. Frost crawled up his right arm. He didn’t speak.
On the platform All Might’s expression had gone tight. Aizawa watched without blinking. In the stands the class had gone still; Midoriya’s pencil hovered over his notebook.
Todoroki exhaled, mist curling. When he moved again the left side stayed lit. Not full. Not yet. But higher than before. The next exchange came with real heat, and Curtis met it without smiling.
“Better,” he said. “Still not enough.”
They clashed again. The temperature in the gym swung wildly while Todoroki fought with more of himself than he had walked in with, and Curtis continued to wait for the moment it actually pushed him.
Todoroki stepped back. The fire died. The ice stopped spreading. He stood with both hands at his sides, breath uneven, the left side of his shirt scorched at the hem.
“I withdraw.”
His voice was quiet, but it carried. “You’re right. I’m not using everything. And I’m not going to force it here just to meet your condition.” He met Curtis’s eyes. “If you stay in the Festival, I’ll face you with both sides when it counts. Not before.”
Curtis nodded once. No mockery. “Fair.”
Todoroki turned and walked off the floor. The stands stayed silent for a beat, then low murmurs started. On the platform Aizawa’s visible eye followed him. All Might’s arms remained crossed. Nezu said nothing.
Curtis rolled his shoulders, scarred hand loose, and looked toward the remaining students still gathered near the entrance. Three challengers. Three withdrawals. The line had thinned, but it hadn’t emptied. He waited to see who, if anyone, would step up next.
No one moved for a moment. Then Monoma stepped forward from the 1-B cluster, grinning like he’d already won.
“I’ll take the next slot. Let’s see if 1-A’s pet Nomu-fighter can handle a copied quirk.”
He never got the chance to copy anything. Curtis crossed the distance, caught his wrist before the touch landed, and set him on the mat with a simple trip. Monoma hit the floor, still smiling, and raised both hands.
“Withdrawing. Data acquired.”
Kendo dragged him off before he could monologue. A Support Course student with a net launcher tried next and watched every shot get walked through. Shinso from General Studies lasted the longest of the remaining first-years—his voice almost caught Curtis once—until Curtis simply stopped listening and closed the gap. Shinso stepped back on his own.
“Not today.”
The line dwindled. A few more tried. None of them pushed him the way the Nomu had. By the time the clock on the far wall hit 5:40, the entrance was empty. Mirio had stayed in the stands the whole time, arms folded, still smiling. Bakugo hadn’t left either. Todoroki stood with his class, expression unreadable.
Curtis turned toward the platform. Nezu, Aizawa, and All Might were still watching.
“Time’s almost up,” he said. “Anyone else?”
The gym stayed quiet. No one stepped forward. The low, steady pull in his chest remained exactly where it always was while the deadline crept closer and the entire school waited to see whether the foreigner who had matched a Nomu would actually walk away from the Festival.
Curtis turned and started for the exit. “Until next time.”
“I’ll fight you!”
Midoriya’s voice cracked across the gym. He was already moving, green sparks flickering at his heels as he dropped from the stands and hit the floor between Curtis and the door. His notebook was gone. His hands were clenched. Eyes bright and determined.
“I’m not letting you leave without trying. You said one person who can push you. I’m that person. Or I’m going to be.”
The stands went still. Bakugo’s eyes narrowed. Todoroki watched without blinking. On the platform All Might’s posture had shifted—proud and worried in the same breath. Aizawa didn’t move to stop it. Nezu’s smile returned, small.
Curtis stopped. He looked at Midoriya—shorter, sparking, refusing to let the deadline pass—and the corner of his mouth twitched.
“You sure? Last one didn’t end well for most of them.”
“I’m sure.” Midoriya set his feet. One For All hummed under his skin. “Come on, Curtis. Push me back.”
Curtis faced him fully. The exit waited behind Midoriya. The clock on the wall kept ticking. The final challenge of the day stood in the center of Gym Gamma, and this time the one asking for it was the boy who had already been told he was strong.
They clashed.
Midoriya closed first, One For All flaring. Curtis met him. The exchange was fast—scarred hand deflecting a kick, Midoriya slipping a counter, both of them moving harder than anything earlier in the afternoon. The stands rose without meaning to.
Midoriya planted. “Delaware Smash!”
The finger-flick detonated at point-blank. Compressed air slammed into Curtis’s chest and launched him backward. He hit the center of the mats and slid, boots carving lines, before he stopped flat on his back in the middle of the arena. Dust settled around him. The gym went silent.
Curtis lay there. The compression shirt was torn at the impact point. He stared at the ceiling for a second, then let out a short, rough breath that might have been a laugh.
Midoriya stood where he’d fired, arm trembling, eyes wide. He hadn’t expected it to land that clean. On the platform All Might’s hand had tightened on the railing. Aizawa’s eye was sharp. Nezu watched without speaking.
Curtis didn’t get up right away. He just stayed on his back in the center of the floor, the low, steady pull in his chest no longer entirely quiet, while the entire school waited to see if the boy who had just put him there had finally pushed him.
Curtis pushed himself up. He rolled his shoulders, dust falling from the torn shirt, and smiled—small, genuine.
“Can you do that a few more times?” He nodded at Midoriya’s already-bruising fingers. “Your arm’s wrecked again. Nice try, Deku. You hit me even harder than the Nomu did.”
The gym stayed quiet. Midoriya’s breath came hard. His hand trembled, the familiar damage already setting in, but his eyes were bright. He hadn’t put Curtis down for good. He had still done what no one else managed.
On the platform All Might’s expression had softened into something proud and pained. Aizawa watched the pair without interrupting. Nezu’s smile was faint.
Curtis walked over and offered his scarred hand. “You pushed me. That’s the condition. I’ll compete.” He met Midoriya’s eyes. “Don’t break yourself proving the rest. Save some for the actual Festival.”
Midoriya stared at the hand, then took it. His grip was shaky. “You’ll really enter?”
“Yeah.” Curtis pulled him steady. “You earned it. Now get to Recovery Girl before that finger locks up.”
The stands finally breathed. The challenge was over. Curtis was in. And the boy who had put him on his back was already being steered toward the medical station while the low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest settled quiet again.
Bakugo hadn’t moved from the edge of the floor. His palms popped, small and constant, smoke curling between his fingers. The anger sat plain on his face—jaw tight, eyes locked on Curtis and Midoriya still standing in the center.
He hadn’t been the one. Deku had. The extra who’d slept through class, the one Bakugo had mounted and blasted and still failed to push, had just been put on his back by the nerd. And now the extra was entering the Festival because of it.
“Damn Deku,” he muttered, low enough that only Kirishima beside him caught it. The explosions didn’t stop. “Damn extra. Both of you.”
Kirishima glanced over, careful. “You still get to fight him on the big stage, man.”
Bakugo’s teeth flashed. “I know.” The pops grew sharper. “And when I do, I’m not holding back like these losers did.”
He turned and stalked toward the exit, shoulders rigid, while the rest of the stands celebrated the result and Midoriya was walked toward Recovery Girl. The anger didn’t fade. It just found a new date.
